   271	                                Appendix II. Some special cases
   272	
   273	      A simple Computer program shows that the only possible counter-examples to the
   274	Jacobian conjecture for polynomials of degrees less than 100 are of degrees (64, 68),
   275	(84, 56), (75, 50) and (99, 66) with some special data attached. A routine computation
   276	shows that the numbers of coefficients of the pairs of polynomials involved are 3370,
   277	5308, 4352 and 7348 respectively. The task of direct verification of the Jacobian con-
   278	jecture by computation for those pairs of polynomials with the said degrees is beyond
   279	the capacity of a large Computer. In this Appendix we shall illustrate some methods to
   280	reduce the numbers of coefficients, and then settle the problems of computations.
